AS200 4/166 upgraded to 233 with a ZLXp-E2 card.  There's a console
environment variable called
TGA_SYNC_GREEN that determines if SOG is enabled on the card.  As I
recall there was no way to do the same under teh ARC firmware, so D-
Unix and VMS would work with an SOG-only monitor but the garbage that
ran under ARC was unable to.

Look for square aspect (4x3) monitors with a native resolution exactly
matching the card you want to use.  Depending on your cards the most
likely would be 1024x768, 1280x1024, and 1600x1200.  Some older 17"
and many 15" LCDs provided the lowest resolution listed.  Many newer
17s, most 19s, and many 20s ran 1280x1024.  Larger, or better 20"
units often came in at 1600x1200 (which is a sweet size to use).  LCDs
are nowhere near as nice to use when being run at other than their
'default' native resolution, unlike CRTs that could adapt their
resolution much more cleanly.

I use an NEC LCD2010X with BNC connectors on my work VAXstation with
SPX card (1280x1024); it works fine on the AS200 and PWS600; the
former requires SOG enabled, the latter does not.  I have a Philips
200P7 (VGA connector, no BNC) at home that works perfectly on the (but
at reduced resolution 1280x1024) and 1600x1200 on a 4D20 card in a
PWS600, as well as the house Macs.  I expect it will also work
perfectly with a Powerstorm 300 or one of the Radeon cards when I
manage to snag a DS15 or DS10 to upgrade the PWS.

You can get adapter cables that plug into a VGA port on a monitor and
pigtail out to three or more BNC sockets that a BNC-terminated video
cable will plug into, if that is useful (it was for me when testing
the Philips monitor on the VAXstation).  As Brian mentioned, if only
the three color connectors are used then you have to have sync-on-
green support in both the card and the monitor.


