PRO hardware, then using an additional (pseudo) device driver
(specifically PI.SYS for an RT-11 UnMapped Monitor and
PIX.SYS for an RT-11 Mapped Monitor) does not in any
way change the rest of the software from being "normal".

Since PI(X).SYS is such a small portion of the total RT-11
software package and its most obvious purpose is to
handle the video monitor (which is very different from
the usual VT100 type terminal), I describe the RT-11
package which runs on a PRO as being "normal" RT-11
including all its advantages and disadvantages.

I also agree that the two disk device drivers, DW(X).SYS
and DZ(X).SYS, are also different from any Qbus or
Unibus device drivers, but that does not alter my opinion
in any way about the rest of the RT-11 software.

The fact that DEC never bothered to add the equivalent
of PI(X).SYS, DW(X).SYS and DZ(X).SYS to allow
RSX-11 to run on a PRO may be due to the limitations
of the PRO with respect to disk, tape and other hardware
which precludes the hardware support which an RSX-11
environment would expect.  But that is another matter and
DEC probably had the technical ability to support RSX-11
on the PRO.

If Johnny can show that the PRO actually requires an RT-11
Monitor file which can't also run under Qbus and Unibus
hardware, then I will change my opinion.  Otherwise, I
agree to disagree with Johnny over what constitutes a
"normal" RT-11 collection of software.

Jerome Fine


