It's been 20+ years since I dealt with this stuff so excuse me if I'm
a bit rusty on the details.

There is a command that will let you mount a file as a logical disk.
Check your RT11 docs for the correct incantation.
Once you've done that, your problems may be only part way solved.

If your disks were written using the RT11 file structure, you should
be able to deal with them with no problems.
Running DUMP against the files, even before you try to mount them will
tell you that.


If they were written in an IBM format, then you will have to write
code to extract the data a block at a time.
I've read and written IBM 8" floppies in the past. If I remember
correctly, they had 1 block of header and then were just consecutive
blocks on the disk.
IBM used them to provide a way to boot some of their systems and load
a full operating system from tape.

Hope this was some help.



