>where FW0 is the device name of the 8" floppy.
>I successfully obtained a set of image files of the diskettes. Then I
>connected the hd to PC, used dd to create a
>full disk image and in the end I mounted this image on SIMH. I can
>read the RT-11 partition,
>and I see all the FLOPPY.xx files.
>The files FLOPPY.00 and FLOPPY.01 are big 988 blocks (exactly as RX02,
>removing track 0).
>I can successfully create single SIMH RX02 images simply doing
>COPY/FILE/DEV FLOPPY.00 DY0:
>The problem is that all other FLOPPY.xx (02 to 29) files are 1216
>blocks, because these diskettes are formatted as IBM diskettes.
>  
>
In the DEC environment on with real DEC hardware (or RX01 / RX02 3rd 
party compatibles),
there are only TWO sizes of 8" floppy diskettes: 494 blocks and 988 
blocks.  The 494 blocks
are always used by the RX01 which must be SSSD 8" diskettes.  I have 
always been led to
believe that the 494 block diskettes are the IBM compatible mode.

The 988 block diskettes are RX02 SSDD 8" diskettes and are formatted as 
M2FM (not sure
exactly of the M2FM designation) with the header (or something or other) 
being in single
density and the actual data being double density.  (Sorry for not being 
able to remember the
exact terminology.)  The RX02 drives are also able to format, write and 
read single density
media.  BUT the RX01 drives can't handle double density media.  The 
actual physical media
are identical, as far as I know.

Under SIMH, I imagine that a block is a block is a block.  I have not 
looked at or used SIMH
with RX01 / RX02 drivers sufficiently to be able to comment.

>These disks contain a BACKUP
>set that I would restore.
>Of course copying it to RX02 device doesn't help, as is is only 500K
>big.
>I tried to copy it to RX33, because it's 1.2MB; RT-11 does it,
>notifying that the destination device is bigger, but
>then BACKUP /RESTORE doesn't work correctly, I suppose because of the
>extra data at the end of the device.
>
>How could I do? Any suggestion?
>  
>
If SIMH allows RT-11 to look at DY0: after you "COPY/FILE/DEV" (probably 
you only are
able to see the first 988 blocks) and the RT-11 file structure is there, 
then why bother with
BACKUP / RESTORE?  If the file structure shows that there are files at 
blocks higher than
block number 988, my only suggestion at this point is to use a device 
which is larger than
988 blocks (such as an RK05) and see what you get after:
COPY/FILE/DEV FLOPPY.02  RK0:
Alternatively, if you understand how logical disks or subsets work,
COPY  FLOPPY.*  DU0:*.*   after which:
MOUNT  LD2:  DU0:FLOPPY.02/NOWRITE
DIR  LD2:/FU/BL/VOL
to see what is there.  NOTE that logical disks or subsets are supported 
starting with V05.00
of RT-11, so if you are using V04.00 or prior, the feature is NOT available.

After you figure out what you have with RT-11 under SIMH using logical 
disks, you can determine
the next step.

BY  THE  WAY,  when you use SIMH, how is your terminal handled?  In 
particular, is the Single
Line Editor (SL:) supported (which requires Application KEYPAD support 
for the PF1, PF2, PF3
and PF3 keys on the keypad)?  When I used SIMH (still using SIMH under 
V2.11 from many years
ago which has partial support), it was quite painful using SL: with the 
terminal software within SIMH.

Jerome Fine


