I don't have much experience with SIMH, so I can't comment on what RQ: 
devices
can't be used.

However, I suggest that you attempt to solve the problem with Ersatz-11 
(aka E11).

The command syntax is a bit different.  However, a couple of hours after 
you read
the manual should be quite sufficient.

IN  PARTICULAR, under E11, it is permissible to:
MOUNT  DY0:  FOOBAR.DSK/RONLY
MOUNT  DU0:  FOOBAR/RONLY

Then under RT-11, you are able to read both devices.  I just tried that 
for a test and
found that:
DIFF/BIN  DY0:  DU0:
produces:  No differences.
leading me to believe that interleave under E11 handles the conversion 
in a completely
transparent manner for the user and that a file MOUNTed (under E11) as a 
device
for use under RT-11 (or any of the other PDP-11 operating systems can be 
used
as a random access block device with block 0 being first and block (n-1) 
being last
for a device with a total of n blocks with all of the other blocks being 
in the expected
order no matter if the software device driver is interleaved (such as 
with DY0:) or not
(as for almost all other hardware devises).

That said, have you (under E11):
MOUNT  DU0:  FLOPPY.02/RONLY
and under RT-11:
DUMP/TERM  DU0:
managed to look at the contents of the file, "FLOPPY.02"?

If you can get Jay to allow you to place to files up at classiccmp 
somewhere (assuming
the content of the file is public domain), then it will be easy to 
download the files and
attempt to figure out the contents.  There may be an RT-11 directory 
imbedded there
and file headers which specify the names and sizes of the files.

By the way, the sizes for 8" floppy media for DEC were:
RX01 = 494 blocks
RX02 = 494 blocks for SSSD and 988 blocks for SSDD
RX03 = 988 blocks for DSSS and 1976 blocks for DSDD
Although DEC never sold an RX03 drive (as far as I know), the code to 
handle double
sided media was present in the V04.00 RT-11 distribution (1980), 
although the optional
code was removed by the time the V05.00 RT-11 distribution (1983) was 
released.  On
the other hand, it seems doubtful that the optional code was actually 
ever used since I seem
to remember that it contained at least one bug that I had to correct 
when I enhanced the
DY.MAC device driver to support double sided media for V05.03 of RT-11 
for use with
the DSD880/30 8" floppy drive and 3 * RL02 hard drive which included 
hardware (both
floppy drive and controller) to support the double sided floppy drive.  
Unfortunately, the
22 bit Qbus address hardware capability was not supported by the 
DSD880/30, so the
only solution was to provide a bounce buffer when the user buffer 
address was higher
than 256 KBytes.

Are these suggestions helpful?


