Forgot to mention: The other problem with a lot of available chips if
you're interfacing to anything modern is that many modern devices
(especially FPGAs) can't take more than 3.3v on their inputs.  There
are a lot of modern microcontrollers which can manage 5v inputs, but
if you think you'll do well bit-banging QBUS with a microcontroller, I
have a nice bridge I can sell you.

The main benefit I see with the 'AS760/641 combo is that it's open-
collector on both sides, and the input switching threshold will work
fine with 3.3v CMOS outputs.  As a result, the FPGA side can be run
with 3.3v pullups while the bus side can run from 5v.  Using something
that runs push-pull on the FPGA side (e.g. AM26S10C) would require a
voltage converter (e.g. 74LVC8T245 is a favorite for us when
interfacing 5v to practically anything) between the bus driver and the
FPGA, which eats up board space and gets expensive quite quickly.  The
other alternative is to put a series resistor in there and use the
FPGA's PCI clamp diodes (they practically all have them) to keep the
voltages in the 3.3v range, but it's a messy solution that can put
quite a bit of strain on the device.


