On Mar 15, 10:07=A0am, jjh <jjhu...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Not sure I follow you concern about the slew rate. Are you saying the
> '760 has a higher slew rate than the original DEC drivers? =A0If so, I
> don't see that as a problem - its signals would be significantly less
> that the minimum setup times. =A0Ringing might be a concern, but if the
> bus is properly terminated then the potential problem would be
> mitigated. =A0If not, putting a small capacitor (10pf) on the output
> would slow the rise time.

Yeah, ringing is the big thing.  I don't think it would be a problem
in a 4/8 slot system, but on a multi-box system with meter-long
cables, it can be a problem (especially because those often aren't
really dreamy in terms of impedance continuity).  So I figure the
'760/641 should be good enough for anything anyone really uses any
more; I figure most of the giant multi-box systems are gone now, since
most experimenters want something they can actually lift or, on the
other end, a UNIBUS system.

> IIRC, the qbus drivers are: 8641 (quad transceivers) 8640 is the bus
> receiver, and 8881 is the bus driver. I can't remember exactly, but I
> thought there is a NS part (DS8222?) that was a very close replacement
> to the 8881?

National had a neat set of drop-in DEC replacement drivers (who do you
think fabbed the OEM ones anyway?) that used an integrator to make
trapezoidal edges with controlled slew rate.  Like all their 120=D9 bus
products, they're long since EOL'ed.  Really nice stuff, but only
reasonable if you can fab your own chips; I looked into doing a
discrete version with op amps (output integrators for OC transistors)
and comparators (inputs with proper thresholds/hysteresis), but the
only quad comparators I found that were fast enough for the receiver
input (30 ns tpd) came out to about $9 per quad package and you'd need
the op amp for the output as well as a bunch of transistors to sink
the current.  Didn't seem too appealing after that; my driver chips
with the '760/'641 combo already come to around $50 in single quantity
builds.

> Some years ago (lot?) I did a qbus interface with the '760 and it
> worked out fine in both 4-slot and 8-slot backplanes.

Good to know.  I think I'll just move forward on that, then; anecdotal
evidence is at least proof that I'm not smoking crack.  And I can
readily acquire those from Mouser.



