Having never seen one in the wild, I can only speculate based on
documentation, but most of DEC's docs seem to think that the backplane
extensions are passive things (though each box obviously has its own
power supply bussing).  The UNIBUS manual in particular has some very
scary TDR trace mockups of a long extended backplane with a number of
impedance discontinuities to hammer home the purpose of the bus
thresholds and hysteresis levels when it comes to undershoot,
overshoot and ringing.

> Yes, so for a four (quad) slot bus it might not take so much.
>
> The 8T97, I believe not so hard to find, seems to sink 48ma.
> I believe that was the popular S-100 bus driver.
>
> The 74367, probably easier to find, sinks 32ma.
> The 74LS367 sinks 24ma. =A0The 74HCT367 can source or sink 35ma.

I was mostly going off DEC's specs to find drivers.  In the KDF11-BA
manual I have, it specifies:

Driver voltage when sinking 70mA: 0.7v max
Driver high leakage current @3.8vDC: 25uA
Driver rise/fall times (10%-90%): 5 ns min

Receiver low threshold: 1.3v max
Receiver high threshold: 1.7v min
Receiver leakage @3.8vDC: 80uA

Common between the two:
tpd: 35 ns max
Skew: 25 ns max
Capacitance load: 10pF

The '760/'741 don't have any problems with most of these (the AS parts
can sink 64mA continuously, no problem, with 100mA max; the ALS can do
24 max, which is not as nice).  The slew rate might be a bit high,
though, and there's no control on it (the earlier National parts
didn't seem to have a specc'ed slew rate either, though I assume they
were probably close enough).

> I don't see a sink current for the DS8641. =A0(The data sheet
> is still on the National web site.)

Indeed.  I guess the DEC spec isn't exactly "you must sink 70mA", but
it seems implicit in the logic-0-level spec.



- Dave


