On Mar 15, 7:19=A0pm, legalize+jee...@mail.xmission.com (Richard) wrote:
> What about using a "Field Programmable Analog Array" like the parts
> described here?
> <http://www.diodes.com/products/semicustom/700_series.html>

Looks interesting, though that's not exactly field programmable; you
can basically design the metal mask layer and have someone fab it.
Cheaper than full custom, but (at least when they do "hardened" FPGAs
like Altera's HardCopy series) you're usually looking at something
like $100K NRE.  This probably isn't on nearly the tiny geometry that
the HardCopy uses, and it's not nearly as large, so maybe $100K is an
order of magnitude off.

Those look like cool parts, and I'll have to remember them for other
projects, but I think it might be a little much up front for a smaller
project.  On the other hand, you could probably design exactly what's
needed (the large NPNs have about 200mA current handling ability, and
with total control over the transistors, you could get the thresholds
just right as well).  The key, of course, would be producing and
selling enough of them at the right price to amortize the cost of
production (which isn't that much once you've got the mask, just like
with PCBs).  If it turned out there was unexpectedly a market for
10,000 of them, it might be cost-effective.


