On Mar 16, 6:23=A0pm, glen herrmannsfeldt <g...@ugcs.caltech.edu> wrote:
> The 74LVC2245A sounds interesting.
>
> Among others, it seems designed to work with lower voltages
> (like many FPGAs) but is tolerant of 5V input/output.
>
> It can sink 50ma, and has built in series termination of 30ohms.
>
> (I found it searching for series termination TTL )

The problem with actual 245 derivatives (as opposed to the '641, which
is an open-collector derivative) is that they do drive high.  With 30-
ohm series termination, that doesn't pose quite as much of a threat
for contention (you're not going to blow anything up), but it will
drive the thevenin-terminated bus (which sits around 2/3 Vcc) higher
than it's intended.  I'd worry about what that does for reflections.

(putting on my thinking cap for once) Of course, the 2245A runs at a
Vcc of 3.3v, which is pretty close to the 3.4-ish that the bus sits
at.  Might be worth looking into... I'd certainly be interested in
spending a few bucks on a tiny board to plug into a bus with various
drivers and running a function generator into them and seeing what
comes out on the scope.  If that turns out well, it'd be very nice;
these are 70 cents in 1-unit quantities instead of 7 bucks.  The '244
equivalent (2244A) is 50 cents, too; useful for multi-purpose boards
where you might be either driving or receiving various generally one-
way signals.


